troduce significant trustworthiness challenges that require dedicated red-teaming methodologies. This paper has presented a comprehensive framework for red-teaming code agents, addressing the unique security and trustworthiness concerns of these systems. Our experiments demonstrate that code agents are vulnerable to various attack vectors, particularly when deployed in autonomous modes with extended operational contexts.\n\nThe trustworthiness assessment of code agents builds upon foundational work in language model evaluation, such as the comprehensive framework established in DecodingTrust [1], while also recognizing the distinct challenges posed by code generation capabilities. As the capabilities of code agents continue to evolve, so too must our red-teaming methodologies, incorporating insights from both AI safety research and software engineering security practices.\n\nThe architectural innovations in deep learning, such as those described in Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition [2], have enabled the complex models underlying modern code agents, but they also introduce new security considerations that must be addressed through careful evaluation and red-teaming.\n\nWe call for continued collaboration between AI safety researchers, software engineers, and security experts to develop robust frameworks for ensuring the safe and trustworthy deployment of code agents. Only through such collaborative efforts can we harness the benefits of AI-powered code generation while minimizing the associated risks.\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[2] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, Jian Sun. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n",
